CloudBurrow patch 90 to github.com/goccy/googlesqlite v0.3.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1458). Applies after patch 0089; needs patches
0036, 0044, 0050, 0062 and 0074.

A table's change table (patch 0036) keeps seven days of changes: each
write's trigger deletes the older ones, and CloudBurrow's front lets go of
what is older than a dataset's time travel window. So once a partition's
last write was let go of, the front could not say when the partition was
last written: INFORMATION_SCHEMA.PARTITIONS' last_modified_time ("the
most recent time that data was written to the partition") went NULL, and
TABLE_STORAGE could not tell active bytes from long-term ones (a partition
"not modified for 90 consecutive days",
https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/pricing#storage). Every change record a
change table lets go of (its START row aside) is now moved, with its
values (a pending record's, patch 0074, from its row in the table), to the
table's kept table, named the table's last path element with
"$kept$changes", by a BEFORE DELETE trigger on the change table, so every
way a record goes keeps it; it is in the database, so it survives a
restart. A kept table is a change table to every reader (no wildcard or
INFORMATION_SCHEMA lists it, and it has none of its own); DROP TABLE drops
it with the change table, and a new history (CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE)
starts an empty one. The engine does not know a table's partitioning; the
front reads a partition's last write from the change table and the kept
table together, and compacts the kept table to the latest record of each
partition. A change table made before this patch gets its kept table and
trigger at the catalog's first sync; what it let go of before then is
gone. change_history_kept_test.go fails without the patch and passes with
it.

